      "problem": "$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3})=$",
      "worked_solution": "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{3}}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{3+1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
